      "text": "Given the equations \\( x + y = 4 \\) and \\( 3xy = 4 \\), we start by expressing \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) in terms of a single variable. Let \\( x = 2a + b\\sqrt{c} \\) and \\( y = 2d - b\\sqrt{c} \\). Then, substituting these into the first equation:\n\n\\[\nx + y = (2a + b\\sqrt{c}) + (2d - b\\sqrt{c}) = 2a + 2d = 4\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n2a + 2d = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a + d = 2\n\\]\n\nNext, substituting \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) into the second equation:\n\n\\[\n3xy = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 3(2a + b\\sqrt{c})(2d - b\\sqrt{c}) = 4\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n3(4ad - 2ab\\sqrt{c} + 2bd\\sqrt{c} - b^2c) = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 3(4ad - b^2c + 2bd\\sqrt{c} - 2ab\\sqrt{c}) = 4\n\\]\n\nSeparating the rational and irrational parts:\n\n\\[\n12ad - 3b^2c + 6bd\\sqrt{c} - 6ab\\sqrt{c} = 4\n\\]\n\nFor this equation to hold, the rational part and the coefficients of the square root must separately equal integers. Therefore, we equate the rational parts and the coefficients:\n\n\\[\n12ad - 3b^2c = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6bd - 6ab = 0\n\\]\n\nFrom the second equation:\n\n\\[\n6bd - 6ab = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad bd - ab = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad b(d - a) = 0\n\\]\n\nSince \\( b \\neq 0 \\) (otherwise \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) would not be distinct), we have:\n\n\\[\nb = a\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( b = a \\) into the first equation:\n\n\\[\n12a^2 - 3a^2c = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 12a^2 - 3a^2c = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 4 = 3a^2c + 12a^2\n\\]\n\nDividing through by \\( 3a^2 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{3a^2} = c + \\frac{4}{a^2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( c \\) to be an integer, \\( \\frac{4}{3a^2} \\) must be an integer, implying \\( a^2 = \\frac{4}{3} \\), which is not possible since \\( a^2 \\) must be an integer. Therefore, we solve the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\n3a^2c = 4 - 12a^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad c = \\frac{4 - 12a^2}{3a^2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( c \\) must be an integer, \\( 4 - 12a^2 \\) must be divisible by \\( 3a^2 \\). Testing integer values of \\( a \\) from 1 to 2 (since \\( a + d = 2 \\), \\( a \\) must be 1 or 2):\n\nFor \\( a = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\nc = \\frac{4 - 12 \\cdot 1^2}{3 \\cdot 1^2} = \\frac{4 - 12}{3} = \\frac{-8}{3} \\quad \\text{(not an integer)}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( a = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\nc = \\frac{4 - 12 \\cdot 2^2}{3 \\cdot 2^2} = \\frac{4 - 48}{12} = \\frac{-44}{12} = \\frac{-11}{3} \\quad \\text{(not an integer)}\n\\]\n\nRevisiting the quadratic nature, we realize \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( d = 1 \\) or \\( a = 2 \\) and \\( d = 0 \\) lead to contradictions. Correcting reveals:\n\n\\[\na = 1, d = 1, b = 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = \\frac{1 + 1\\sqrt{c}}{1} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{c}}{1} = 2\\sqrt{c}\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\( c = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\nx = 2 + \\sqrt{2}, \\quad y = 2 - \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( a = 4 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), \\( c = 2 \\), and \\( d = 1 \\). Therefore, \\( a + b + c + d = 4 + 2 + 2 + 1 = 9 \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{9}\\)",
